{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2399 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bc8e7339c7b5a9920a1d76aa17d422a5022c0367ea97cf56250a3e760e26e413" :edb/db-before-t 2398 :edb/db-after-t 2399 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535711 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:45:56.553Z" 13194139535711 true] [17592186164486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164488 13194139535711 true] [17592186164489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164491 13194139535711 true] [17592186164492 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164493 13194139535711 true] [17592186164495 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164497 13194139535711 true] [17592186164506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164507 13194139535711 true] [17592186166482 1100 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6feea8db62000e44334251a96c39bd8d0d8d7b1ec9a61380f3a5b4b613ea6c80.png)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." 13194139535711 true] [17592186166482 1100 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n${x^{′}(t) =-y(t); y^{′}(t) = 9x(t)$\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T$, respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." 13194139535711 false] [17592186166482 1145 "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/bceb494a6baeca8695157b4fd285b2880a242367747c0d72a0f573612ef87652.png)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)" 13194139535711 true] [17592186166482 1145 "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0$, we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x =-\\sin (3t); y = 3\\cos (3t)\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/53de9c32f79277beb9f683133f7b0cf5.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mid \\sin(3t) \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mid \\cos(3t) \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/3d7278b105e7e6f65eb433ca671c6a4e.png)" 13194139535711 false] [17592186166483 1100 "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/6b0386ae3c66a70a85c39b440c659e81.png)" 13194139535711 false] [17592186166483 1100 "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" 13194139535711 true] [17592186166483 1145 "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}\n$$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{complex}(t) &= ve^{(a + b\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (bt))(p + q\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt)p - \\sin (bt)q) + \\textrm{i}e^{at}(\\sin (bt)p + \\cos (bt)q)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = c_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + c_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0$, we require $a < 0$." 13194139535711 false] [17592186166483 1145 "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$" 13194139535711 true] [17592186166484 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." 13194139535711 true] [17592186166484 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}$. Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." 13194139535711 false] [17592186166484 1145 "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}\n$$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{complex}(t) &= ve^{(a + b\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (bt))(p + q\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt)p - \\sin (bt)q) + \\textrm{i}e^{at}(\\sin (bt)p + \\cos (bt)q)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = c_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + c_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\na = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ne^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $t$. Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin.\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0\n$$\n\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is a center." 13194139535711 false] [17592186166484 1145 "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center." 13194139535711 true] [17592186166873 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362673 13194139535711 true] [17592186166873 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362706 13194139535711 true] [17592186166874 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362742 13194139535711 true] [17592186166875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362768 13194139535711 true] [17592186166875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362787 13194139535711 true] [17592186166875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362799 13194139535711 true] [17592186166875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362811 13194139535711 true] [17592186167180 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362762 13194139535711 true] [17592186167180 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362765 13194139535711 true] [17592186167180 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535711 true] [17592186167180 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/79017dcca6805220acfca563829e1feb28e00520ca095cdb4f19f0f16223aff8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =